+# Local MAPF core feasibility results
+
+This directory records the opt-in `LocalConflictResolver` experiment. It is a
+bounded prioritized joint search for at most four participants, not complete
+MAPF and not a production activation of centralized planning.
+
+## Outcome
+
+All six physically feasible generic single-lane scenarios passed through the
+local resolver: 1v1, dynamic same-direction third agent, dynamic
+opposite-direction third agent, 2v1, 1v2, and 2v2. Every returned proposal is a
+normal `map<ParticipantId, Plan>` at the `CentralizedNegotiation` API boundary.
+The tests check original-goal completion, pairwise trajectory conflict, forced
+reverse, timeout, holding use, and dynamic replanning.
+
+The local path used zero negotiation tables and zero rollout alternatives. The
+baseline required 2--5 tables but no rollout on this feasible staging topology.
+The local path was faster in these runs, but it was more conservative: its
+makespan and usually its total delay were higher. This establishes bounded
+core feasibility and table-count reduction, not universal superiority over
+the existing negotiation algorithm.
+
+`metrics.csv` contains the A/B values and `ab-summary.log` is a compact raw
+run record. Timings are single-run wall-clock observations and should not be
+treated as a benchmark distribution.
+
+## Algorithm exercised
+
+The resolver first plans independent paths using each agent's existing RMF
+`Planner`. When those conflict, it enumerates bounded priority orders and a
+topology-driven holding macro:
+
+1. route one participant to any reachable graph-declared holding point;
+2. preserve that participant's holding occupancy;
+3. plan the other participants in priority order, optionally preserving a
+   wait prefix at a non-passthrough current waypoint;
+4. resume the yielding participant from the holding point to its original
+   goal;
+5. reject any complete set with pairwise trajectory or persistent terminal
+   conflict.
+
+There are no robot names, participant counts, waypoint IDs, corridor IDs, or
+cardinal directions in the resolver. Search is bounded by configurable agent,
+high-level-node, low-level-replan, total-time, and per-low-level-call limits.
+Any unsupported, infeasible, interrupted, or budget-exceeded result falls back
+to the existing `CentralizedNegotiation` table algorithm.
+
+## Persistent destination occupancy
+
+An earlier inline-parking topology put both the waiting starts and final goals
+on the only through route. Its 2v2 case remained unsolved after 30 seconds and
+the fallback expanded 77 tables and 1793 rollout alternatives. The rejection
+was correct: a robot stopped at its destination physically blocked another
+robot's goal. The implementation does not delete terminal occupancy or move an
+idle robot. Such layouts need additional parking capacity or a relocation
+policy and remain separate from local conflict resolution.
+
+## Production call graph finding
+
+`CentralizedNegotiation::solve()` is only the opt-in feasibility boundary used
+here. The production fleet adapter does not call it. Normal `GoToPlace`
+planning creates one `services::FindPath`, while negotiation enters
+`GoToPlace::Active::_respond()`, creates one `services::Negotiate`, expands
+per-participant `jobs::Planning`/`jobs::Rollout`, and submits that one
+participant's itinerary through the table responder. The approval callback
+then starts `ExecutePlan`.
+
+A production joint insertion therefore needs a boundary that owns the current
+start, goal, planner, generation/cancellation state, and atomic plan approval
+for every participant in the local conflict set. The solver and result type can
+remain in `rmf_traffic`, and no new ROS wire message is inherently required for
+an in-process implementation. However, the existing adapter `_respond` API
+only owns one participant's intent, so production activation cannot be done by
+an `rmf_traffic`-only switch without changing that caller/commit boundary.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+From this worktree:
+
+```bash
+source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
+cmake --build build/traffic-tests -j2
+build/traffic-tests/test_rmf_traffic "[local_mapf]"
+RMF_SINGLE_LANE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=8 \
+  build/traffic-tests/test_rmf_traffic "[local_mapf_ab]"
+RMF_SINGLE_LANE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=8 \
+  build/traffic-tests/test_rmf_traffic "[single_lane][diagnostic]"
+```
+
+Set `RMF_SINGLE_LANE_SCENARIO` to one of `1v1`,
+`1v1_dynamic_same`, `1v1_dynamic_opposite`, `2v1`, `1v2`, or `2v2` to run a
+single local scenario.
